139	Coastal Ecology, Antarctica	This project is being carried out through an agreement between the Argentine\nAntarctic Institute (IAA); the University of Mar del Plata, Argentina; Navy\nHydrographic Survey, Argentina; University of Bahia Blanca, Argentina;\nUniversity of Rosario, Argentina; and Scientific Research and High Education\nCenter of Ensenada, Mexico.\n\nProject contacts for specific disciplines are listed below:\n   Project Chief:     Jose Francisco Gallo\n   Oceanography:      Silvia Ines Romero\n   Oceanography:      Walter Hugo Grismeyer\n   Biology:           Fabrizio Oyarbide\n   Biology:           Silvia Rodriguez\n   Tide Measurement:  Monica Adriana Stadelmann\n   Chemical Analysis: Patricia Liliana Castro\n   Chemical Analysis: Eduardo Mondino\n   Solar Radiation:   Eduardo Luccini\n   Ocean Optics:      Martin Montes	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214608383-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.726775
140	Longterm upper Air Ozone Soundings in Antarctica from the German Antarctic Station since 1985.	Since 1985 regular ozone soundings have been performed at Georg Forster\n                                                Station (70#46'S, 11#41'E). In the beginning of 1992, this ozone sounding\n                                                program was moved to Neumayer (70#39'S, 8#15'W), which is located at the\n                                                same latitude 750 km further west. The results of both stations can be\n                                                regarded as one timeseries, now covering a whole decade with only minor\n                                                interruptions.\n                                                \n                                                Vertical atmospheric soundings are performed about weekly. They include profile\n                                                measurements of ozone partial pressure, air pressure, temperature, relative\n                                                humidity and wind vector. Usually the profiles start at ground level and end\n                                                between 15 and 37 km.\n                                                \n                                                The data obtained at Georg-Forster Station include mainly the mean pressure\n                                                levels while the data from Neumayer have a mean height resolution of about\n                                                50 meter.\n                                                \n                                                At AWI the ozone soundings of both stations are archived in a relational\n                                                database (SYBASE). Up to now only the data from Neumayer Station are available\n                                                online on WWW.\n                                                \n                                                Actual soundings normally reach the database once a year.\n                                                \n                                                The data can be retrieved with Web and are free of charge. The address\n                                                is: 	dataset	https://www.awi.de/nc/en/science/long-term-observations/atmosphere/antarctic-neumayer/meteorology.html	NASA Earthdata	C1214598346-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.73061
141	Surface Radiation Measurements at the Neumayer Ground Station (Antarctica)	Since March 1982 surface radiation balance measurements have been carried out\n                                                at the German Antarctic Georg-von-Neumayer Station of the Alfred Wegener\n                                                Institute for Polar and Marine Research (AWI).\n                                                      \n                                                The radiation signals are taken at one minute intervals and stored as averages\n                                                over 10 minutes. They include:\n                                                      \n                                                - global radiation (pyranometer CM11, Kipp+Zonen),\n                                                - reflected solar radiation (pyranometer CM11, Kipp+Zonen),\n                                                - downward long-wave radiation (pyrradiometer Lange),\n                                                - upward long-wave radiation (pyrradiometer Lange),\n                                                - sunshine duration (Solar 111, Haenni).\n                                                      \n                                                In March 1992 the radiation measurements at Neumayer were extended in order to\n                                                fulfill the standards of the Baseline Surface Radiation Network (BSRN). The\n                                                pyrradiometer were replaced by pyrgeometers (PIR Eppley), the averaging period\n                                                was reduced to 5 minutes, and the  following quantities are now additionally\n                                                measured:\n                                                      \n                                                - diffuse sky radiation (pyranometer CM11, Kipp+Zonen),\n                                                - direct solar radiation (NIP, Eppley mounted on a sun tracker),\n                                                - OG1 (global>530nm, pyranometer CM11, Kipp+Zonen),\n                                                - RG8 (global>695nm, pyranometer CM11, Kipp+Zonen),\n                                                - UV (300-370nm, TUVR, Eppley).\n                                                      \n                                                The pyranometers and pyrgeometers are ventilated with slightly preheated air to\n                                                minimize hoar frost problems and zero offsets during cloudless and windless\n                                                conditions. The instruments were maintained continuously several times per day\n                                                throughout the year. After one year of operation they are re-calibrated at the\n                                                German Weather Bureau according the World Radiometric Reference (WRR).\n                                                      \n                                                At AWI, the radiation measurements - averaged over 10 & 5 minutes - are\n                                                archived together with the data from the mast measurements, daily upper air\n                                                soundings and routine synoptic surface observations in a relational database\n                                                (SYBASE). Additionally some derived quantities (Iqbal 1983) such as the solar\n                                                elevation, extraterrestrial solar insolation, astronomical sunshine duration\n                                                are available.\n                                                      \n                                                In order to obtain daily averaged radiation data, each day was subdivided into\n                                                eight 3-hourly intervals (0-3, 3-6, ..., 21-24 UTC). For each 3-hourly interval\n                                                an average was derived irrespective of the number of measurements. This\n                                                procedure was chosen in order to minimize problems resulting from missing\n                                                values.  If one or more 3-hourly averages were missing, no daily average was\n                                                calculated.\n                                                      \n                                                Daily averaged radiation data are available from 13 March 1982 till 28 February\n                                                1994. They can be retrieved directly via World Wide Web:\n                                                'http://www.bremerhaven.de/sixcms/detail.php?id=13742'.	dataset	https://www.awi.de/en/science.html	NASA Earthdata	C1214585734-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.735022
142	Magnetic observations collected from Antarctica and Macquarie Island since 1952	Final one minute average values of the absolute geomagnetic field in the north (X), east (Y) and vertical (Z) components in units of nanoTesla (nT).\n\nMagnetic variometer data have been collected at Macquarie Is. since 1952; Mawson since 1955; and Casey since 1988. Data were not digital from Macquarie Is. until late 1984; from Mawson until late 1986. They have been digital from Casey since 1988. Data that are currently available from the GA website are not complete, but improving. The status of data availability is available on the website.\n\nParticular magnetic elements can be chosen to be plotted or tabulated data are available.	dataset	http://www.ga.gov.au/scientific-topics/positioning-navigation/geomagnetism	NASA Earthdata	C1214311742-AU_AADC	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.739593
143	Adelie Penguin Dietary Data From Bechervaise Island Antarctica	This dataset contains the results from surveys on the feeding habits of Adelie Penguins (Pygoscelis adeliae) on Bechervaise Island, Mawson, Antarctica.  Surveys have been conducted since 1991, and are ongoing to determine the diet composition and prey species of penguins.\n\nData for this project were compiled by Megan Tierney, as part of her PhD Thesis, and are presented in two excel spreadsheets.\n\nAlso provided in the Related URL section, is a link to a trophic database of "A compilation of dietary and related data from the Southern Ocean".  This database contains a large amount of other publicly available diet related data collected as part of the Australian Antarctic program.	dataset	https://data.aad.gov.au/eds/3173/download	NASA Earthdata	C1214311760-AU_AADC	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.743474
147	Microprobe and noble gas analyses of rock and meteoritic debris at Graphite Peak, Antarctica.	Detailed microprobe data of the major and minor elements from samples collected\n      at Graphite Peak Antarctica.  Datasets include all Fosterite and Enstatite\n      analyses, metal grains, meteoritic metal, platinum group elements (PGEs), Fe-Ni\n      oxides, opaques, silicates, sulfides, SiAl.  In addition, noble gas\n      measurements on magnetic grains and carbon carriers (fullerenes) is included.\n      SEM and Microprobe picture are available for individual magnetic grains and\n      meteroritc fragments identified in these samples.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214586474-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.759866
148	BIOSTRATIGRAPHY AND SEDIMENTOLOGY OF THE SANTONIAN-MAASTRICHTIAN FROM THE JAMES ROSS BASIN, ANTARCTICA. PALEOBIOLOGICAL AND PALEOENVIRONMETAL EVENTS-PICTO 36326-2005	ENGLISH\nThis proposal is directed to the study of important, but not yet totally solved, scientific questions involving geologic, paleobiologic, and paleoclimatic high-latitude events. The expected results will also have application in exploration activities requiring the utilization of high-resolution chronological charts. The excellent sedimentary record of the James Ross Basin, with thick, fossiliferous, and relatively complete Santonian-Danian and Eocene sections, is particularly apt for the study of global changes involved in the transition from the warm-temperate Cretaceous climatic conditions to the severe, cold Neogene climatic conditions. It is hypothesized that the faunal composition and geographic and stratigraphic distribution of the Late Cretaceous marine fossils (mega- and microfossils) should reflect the interaction of geologic, climatic, and biologic events associated to the splitting of Gondwana fragments and southern high-latitudes climatic and oceanographic changes. The knowledge of these interactions is hampered by the lack of a precise temporal and spatial ordering of geologic events and by an inadequate knowledge of the foraminiferal associations and paleo-environmental settings within the Basin, particularly near the transition of the major Santonian- Danian sedimentary cycles and the Eocene. The establishment of a detailed chronology, based on ammonite and foraminifera bio-stratigraphy, and the knowledge of the geologic evolution, paleo-ecology, sedimentary facies and paleoenvironmental settings, and sequence stratigraphy for the Santonian-Danian interval are one of the major expected results of this proposal. The expected faunal changes and events within the James Ross Basin will be precisely framed within this biostratigraphic scheme, and compared with faunal changes already known from Tierra del Fuego and ODP sites near Antarctica. During the Late Cretaceous and the Eocene, Antarctica and Tierra del Fuego were closer together than their present geographic position, thus a transect from shallow marine, typical for the James Ross Basin, to deep marine environments, typical for most of the Tierra del Fuego succession, could be evaluated.\n\nSPANISH\n\nSe propone estudiar un conjunto de acontecimientos geol�gicos, paleobiol�gicos y paleoclim�ticos desarrollados en altas latitudes del Hemisferio Sur, cuyo conocimiento es importante para resolver problemas cient�ficos con aplicaci�n en exploraci�n de recursos naturales que requieran la utilizaci�n de esquemas cronol�gicos de alta resoluci�n. El excelente registro sedimentario de la cuenca James Ross, con espesas secciones fosil�feras, relativamente continuas para el Santoniano-Daniano y el Eoceno, favorecen el estudio de cambios ambientales globales que involucran la transici�n entre las condiciones c�lidas y templadas del Cret�cico y las severas condiciones clim�ticas fr�as del Ne�geno. La investigaci�n de la composici�n, distribuci�n geogr�fica y estratigr�fica de f�siles (mega y microfauna) del Cret�cico superior deber�a reflejar la interacci�n de acontecimientos geol�gicos, clim�ticos y biol�gicos relacionados con la fragmentaci�n de Gondwana y los cambios clim�ticos y oceanogr�ficos ocurridos en altas latitudes del Hemisferio Sur. El estudio de estas interacciones est� dificultado por la falta de un adecuado ordenamiento espacial y temporal de acontecimientos y por la falta de conocimiento m�s detallado de las asociaciones de foramin�feros y paleoambientes sedimentarios, especialmente en las transiciones entre los grandes ciclos sedimentarios del Santoniano-Daniano y del Eoceno\ndefinidos en la cuenca. La obtenci�n de un adecuado marco cronol�gico de referencia, basado en la bioestratigraf�a de los amonites y los foramin�feros, y el entendimiento de la evoluci�n geol�gica, paleoecol�gica, de facies y paleoambientes sedimentarios y estratigraf�a secuencial del intervalo Santoniano-Maastrichtiano, imprescindible para abordar correctamente estas interacciones, constituye uno de los objetivos fundamentales del presente proyecto. Otro objetivo general es la comparaci�n de la\ncolumna del Cret�cico superior de Ant�rtida con la sucesi�n sedimentaria, recientemente establecida por el grupo de investigaci�n en la Isla Grande de Tierra del Fuego y con las determinadas en sitios del programa ODP (Ocean Drilling Project) en regiones ant�rticas. Dada la mayor proximidad geogr�fica de las regiones ant�rticas y fueguinas durante el Cret�cico tard�o, y el contraste generalizado de sus respectivos ambientes depositacionales (plataforma en la cuenca James Ross y marino profundo en Tierra del\nFuego) permitir� evaluar una suerte de transecta comparativa entre ambientes de plataforma y marinos profundos para el Cret�cico superior. Por razones de continuidad estratigr�fica se contempla	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214594404-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.763643
149	GEOPHYSICAL INVESTIGATIONS OF THE MAGMATIC ARC IN THE NORTHWESTERN PENINSULA ANTARCTICA AND ITS TECTONIC DEVELOPMENT - PICTO 36177 2005	ENGLISH\nThis project comprises geophysical, structural, and petrologic studies aiming to define the geometry, lateral relationships, and tectonic evolution of the geologic units of the Mesozoic magmatic arc of the western Antarctic Peninsula. The obtained results will be correlated with the southernmost South America (Tierra del Fuego) which would have been part of the same Mesozoic magmatic arc. Processing of geophysical data (gravimetry, magnetic data, geoelectric data and reflection seismic) along with the petrologic study of plutonic, volcanic, and metamorphic rocks, accompanied by specific paleomagnetic and AMS surveys in some volcanic, intrusive and sedimentary bodies, will allow us to present maps with geologic and geophysical sections. Modeling of geophysical data will allow us to decipher the deep structural geometry of the western Antarctic Peninsula, whereas the surface structural survey will be directed towards the characterization of deformation mechanisms (both in the brittle and ductile regime) as well as the structural evolution of the studied area. The planned paleomagnetic studies aim to address the oroclinal vs. orogenic nature of the Antarctic Peninsula arc. Paleogeographic reconstructions from the Early Jurassic through Upper Cenozoic will be presented as well as balanced cross-sections with the pre-compressive configuration with the purpose of defining the geometry and paleogeographic boundaries of the old retro-arc and fore-arc basins.\n\nSPANISH\nEl proyecto comprende escencialmente un estudio integral geofisico estructural y petrologico que apunta a definir la geometria,relaciones laterales y su desarrollo tectonico en superficie y en profundidad de las diferentes unidades geologicas del antiguo arco magmatico Mesozoico parcialmente aflorantes en el borde W de la Peninsula Antartica.Los resultados obtenidos en este sector antartico, seran correlacionados con el extremo de Sudamerica en el sector de Tierra del Fuego que habria formado parte de ese arco magmatico durante el Mesozoico.La elaboracion de los datos geofisicos(gravimetria,magnetometria, geoelectrica y sismica de reflexion). Estudios petrologicos de las rocas plutonicas, volcanicas y metamorficas,paleomagneticos y de anisotropia de susceptibilidad magnetica, especificos sobre cuerpos volcanicos, intrusivos y sedimentarios, permitiran presentar mapas y transectas geologicas y geofisicas que sinteticen la evolucion geodinamica del arco magmatico integrando estos resultados con la informacion del sector sudamericano.Todos los datos a adquirir en el terreno, seran georreferenciados con DGPS . La aplicacion 1 de 9 de modelos de inversion a los relevamientos geofisicos permitiran fundamentalmente reconstruir la geometria estructural profunda del borde W de la Peninsula Antartica, mientras que el relevamiento estructural apuntara a la determinacion de los mecanismos deformativos tanto en regimen ductil como fragil y contribuiran a la reconstruccion de la historia deformativa de la region. Los estudios paleomagneticos aqui propuestos coadyudaran a dilucidar si el arqueamiento de la Peninsula Antartica es primario u oroclinal o bien controlado por una serie de rotaciones locales que configuran la forma actual. Se realizaran reconstrucciones paleogeograficas desde el Jurasico temprano hasta el Cenozoico superior y perfiles balanceados estirados reconstruyendo las dimensiones pre-compresion con el fin de estimar geometria y limites paleogeograficos de la antigua cuenca de retro y antearco y espesores del relleno sedimentario afectado por la orogenia.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214594402-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.769527
150	Biodiversity and ecology of algae from continental environments in Maritime Antarctica. Project No. 25  2002-2004	ENGLISH:\nProject No. 25 2002-2004 (in cooperation with Buenos Aires University,\nArgentina)\n\nThis project aims to obtain a thorough survey of the diversity of continental\nalgae in three areas of the Maritime Antarctica: Hope Bay, Cierva Point and\nPotter Cove. Environmental features influencing both structure and functioning\nof freshwater algal communities (phytoplankton, phytoperiphyton) are analyzed. \nInteractions between aquatic communities such as herbivory and competition are\nalso studied, thus contributing to the knowledge of the integrated functioning\nof these communities.  On the other hand, the composition and structure of soil\nalgal communities from Cierva Point is exhaustively studied.  On the basis of\nprevious results obtained by our group, species or groups of species requiring\ndeeper taxonomical studies through culturing and/or molecular techniques will\nbe selected. Polimorphism of certain taxa will also be studied, together with\nphysico-chemical factors involved.\n\nSPANISH:\nDiversidad y ecologia de algas de ambientes continentales de Antartica\nMaritima.Proyecto No 25  2002-2004 (En cooperacion con Universidad de Buenos\nAires)\n\nEste proyecto apunta a obtener un relevamiento exhaustivo de la diversidad\nalgal continental en tres zonas de la Antartica Maritima: Bahia Esperanza,\nPunta Cierva y Caleta Potter.  En el marco de este proyecto se analizan las\ncondiciones ambientales que influyen en la estructura y funcionamiento de las\ncomunidades algales (fitoplancton y fitoperifiton).  Las investigaciones\npropustas apuntan tambien a analizar las interacciones entre las comunidades\nacuaticas: efectos de herbivoria y fenomenos de competencia, asi como a obtener\nun conocimiento integrado del funcionamiento de las comunidades acuaticas de\nestos sistemas.  Por otro lado se efectuara un relevamiento completo de la\nficoflora del suelo en la zona de Punta Cierva, estudiandose en forma\nparticular aquellas especies que presenten interes ficologico.  Ademas, en base\na la informacion obtenida previamente por nuestro grupo en estas zonas\nantarticas, se seleccionaran algunos taxa clave de estas comunidades cuyas\nidentificaciones requieren estudios mas profundos mediante cultivos y tecnicas\nmoleculares. Tambien se estudiara el polimorfismo de ciertos taxa y los\nfactores fisico-quimicos involucrados.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214613597-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.774322
151	Breeding Biology of Male Southern Elephant Seals (Mirounga leonine) at South Shetland Islands, Antarctica. Project No. 44 2002-2004	Project: Breeding Biology of Male Southern Elephant Seals (Mirounga\nleonine) at South Shetland Islands, Antarctica. Project No. 44 2002-2004\n\nENGLISH\nProject in cooperation with Universidad Nacional de La Plata, Buenos\nAires, ARGENTINA\n\nThe Southern elephant seal is a polygynous, sexual dimorphic species\nin which males are 4 -10 times greater than females. Males begin to\narrive at the start of the breeding season and  fight each other in\nareas where females will settle to give birth. When pregnant females\nbegin to arrive, they gather in groups called harems. The result of\nthe encounters between males is a dominance hierarchy at each breeding\narea, which reduces access to the grouped females to the\nhighest-ranking males, thus allowing them to increase their breeding\nsuccess. Male size is an important variable to take into account in\nthis social structure; however, additional factors such as prior\nresidence at the breeding area, male age, and time spent on the beach\ncould also be variables that affect male social rank in the dominance\nhierarchy and thus male breeding success.\n\nBy combining different techniques (paternity analyses, body\nmeasurements, daily censuses) we propose to study the breeding biology\nof southern elephant seals at Stranger Point, King George Island,\nidentifying potential different strategies displayed by males to\nincrease their breeding success.\n\nSPANISH\nBiologia reproductiva de machos de elefante marino del sur (Mirounga\nleonina) en las Islas Shetland del Sur, Antartica. (Proyecto en\ncooperacion con la Universidad Nacional de La Plata)\n   Director: Lic. Alejandro Carlini\n   Co-Director:  ---\n\nResumen del Proyecto: El elefante marino del sur es una especie\npoligamica y sexualmente dimorfica en la cual los machos son entre 4 y\n10 veces mas pesados que las hembras. Durante la estacion reproductiva\nlos machos adultos compiten por el acceso a las hembras y como\nresultado de esta competencia se establece una jerarquizacion de los\nindividuos dentro y en las proximidades de los harenes. El tamano\nparece ser una variable de gran importancia en dicha jerarquizacion\npero otros factores, entre ellos el arribo temprano a la colonia, la\nedad y el tiempo de permanencia tambien podrian afectarla y por lo\ntanto hacer lo propio con el exito reproductivo. Ademas es posible que\nmachos de diferentes jerarquias adopten distintas estrategias con el\nobjeto de maximizar la probabilidad de fecundar hembras.\n\nCombinando diferentes metodologias (analisis de paternidad por\nmicrosatelites, medidas corporales, analisis de sonogramas y\nmonitoreos periodicos a lo largo de la estacion reproductiva) se\npropone estudiar la biolog?a reproductiva de los machos de la especie,\nen la colonia situada en Punta Stranger, Isla 25 de Mayo, a fin de\nidentificar las posibles estrategias individuales empleadas por ellos\npara maximizar su exito reproductivo.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214614057-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:43:28.778487
