179	Cosmogenic nuclide data for bedrock samples from the Ford Ranges, Marie Byrd Land, West Antarctica	This data set consists of cosmogenic nuclide concentrations in samples from\n      bedrock surfaces in the Ford Ranges, Marie Byrd Land, West Antarctica. These\n      surfaces have been exposed by ice retreat since the last glacial maximum,\n      approximately 10,500 years ago. However, the majority of samples contain\n      nuclide concentrations indicating much longer and more complicated exposure\n      histories.\n      \n      The primary data are concentrations of the cosmic-ray-produced nuclides Be-10\n      and Al-26 measured in quartz, and Cl-36 measured in K-feldspar, sample\n      locations, and the duration of recent exposure inferred from measurements on\n      co-existing glacial erratics. In addition, the data set contains detailed\n      information about the sites and samples, chemical compositions of the minerals\n      analyzed and compositions of the host rocks, as required to compute nuclide\n      production rates.  This information is provided so that the data can be\n      re-interpreted if nuclide production rates or correction factors are revised in\n      future.	dataset	http://www.usap-dc.org/view/dataset/600002	NASA Earthdata	C1214615474-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:23.553354
180	Exposure ages of glacial deposits and bedrock, Scott Glacier, Antarctica	This data set consists of exposure ages for glacial deposits on nunataks and ice-free areas adjacent to Scott Glacier, Transantarctic Mountains. Most samples are glacially transported clasts deposited during the last glacial period or exposed by subsequent retreat of the glacier, ca. 16,000 yr B.P. to present. These ages are based on concentrations of the cosmic-ray-produced nuclide Be-10. There are also data from bedrock samples on Mt Rigby, which have longer and more complicated exposure histories. For these samples, concentrations of the cosmogenic nuclides Al-26 and Be-10, and associated "ages" are the result of repeated exposure punctuated with periods of ice cover and may have been affected by glacial erosion.  Apparent ages for the bedrock samples should not be taken as indicative of prolonged single-stage exposure.  The primary data are sample locations and exposure ages. Ages are calculated using currently accepted nuclide production rates and correction factors, as described in the footnotes. The data set also contains chemical and isotopic data needed to re-compute exposure ages if nuclide production rates or correction factors are revised in future.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214598050-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:23.565418
181	Battye Glacier Formation Paleontology and Sedimentology, Beaver Lake Region, Antarctica	Glacimarine strata of the Battye Glacier Formation (c.130 m thick),\n      Pagodroma Group, exposed in the Amery Oasis of East Antarctica,\n      provide a record of late Miocene palaeoenvironmental conditions in an\n      ice-distal setting. The formation overlies the Amery Erosion Surface\n      (c.300 m to c.270 m above sea level) that formed during an advance of\n      the Lambert Glacier, at least 750 km further north than today, into\n      Prydz Bay (ODP Site 739). Two lithological members: a grey and muddier\n      Lower Member and a brown, sand-rich Upper Member, reflect variation in\n      proximity to the terminus of the Lambert Glacier. Ice-distal,\n      glacimarine, diatom-bearing mud (up to 12% biogenic silica) and in\n      situ articulated molluscs occur in the Lower Member. Similar Neogene\n      strata are known on land only from the Pliocene Sorsdal Formation in\n      the Vestfold Hills, near the Antarctic coast. The Battye Glacier\n      Formation is significant because of its inland location, which\n      indicates that ice-distal marine conditions existed 250 km inland from\n      the current Amery Ice Shelf edge. Three stratigraphic intervals of\n      diatom-bearing mud are recognized from glacially reworked clasts and\n      from in situ strata informally referred to as the McLeod Beds and\n      Bed A.\n      \n      The diatom-bearing mud also contains sponge spicules and minor\n      silicoflagellates and ebridians. Marine diatom biostratigraphy\n      constrains the age of the beds to between 10.7 and 9.0 Ma (late\n      Miocene). Abundant benthic diatoms suggest deposition within shallow\n      euphotic depths. The high abundance of intercalary valves of Eucampia\n      antarctica from an interval of the McLeod Beds suggests that there was\n      less winter sea-ice than in Prydz Bay today. It is unlikely that\n      sea-ice was perennial because the presence of Thalassionema spp. and\n      Stellarima stellaris (Roper) Hasle et Sims suggests that summer\n      sea-surface temperatures were too warm (> 0 degrees C and > 3 degrees\n      C respectively). The palaeoclimate at the time of deposition appears\n      to have been analogous to that in modern fjords of East Greenland\n      (e.g. Kangerdlugssuaq Fjord), which is consistent with the\n      depositional model proposed previously for the Pagodroma Group. The\n      three diatom-bearing mud intervals were deposited in the Amery Oasis,\n      c.250 km inland of the current Amery Ice Shelf edge, when the East\n      Antarctic Ice Sheet was reduced in size relative to today.\n      \n      Further information can be found in reference:\n      \n      Whitehead, J.M., Harwood, D.M. and McMinn, A., 2003. Ice-distal upper\n      Miocene marine strata from inland Antarctica. Sedimentology v. 50,\n      22pp.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214613460-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:24.393358
182	Marine Mammal Monitoring Data for Antarctica from the Instituto Antartico Argentino	The objective to be reached by these projects is to improve quality\nand objectivity in planning efforts of conservation in Antarctica\nthrough defining strategies of rational resource management.\n\nMonitoring of selected predator species like penguins and seals has\nbeen doing since the last ten years in Antarctica. This information is\nuseful not only for fisheries management but also to know how the\nglobal climate change progress is affecting the ecosystem.\n\nThe idea to carry out related studies on Antarctica and Patagonia were\nbegun in 1979 when we started to work with the comparative study of\nthe population dynamic of southern elephant seal as a predator in a\nbig scale from Antarctica and Patagonia.  The original data was\nanalized in the University of Minnesota in 1981.\n\nTo continue working in this way we have formalized a multidisciplinary\ngroup formed by several institutions as National Parks Administration,\nWildlife Service of Rio Negro State, Argentine Center for\nRadiocommunication Studies and Electromagnetic Compatibility\n(CAERCEM), and Sea World Foundation.  In international order it is\nincluded Alfred Wegener Institute (AWI) and European Space Agency\n(ESA).\n\nThe researchers Stanganelli,  Z. and  Vergani, D. have concluded\ntheir work for the Instituto Antartico Argentino in 1997 .	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214611495-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:24.431114
183	Use of Infrared Sensors as a New Method in Estimating Wilson's Storm Petrels' Population Density in Antarctica	As a new method for localization of Wilson's Storm Petrel Oceanites\noceanicus (Oceanitidae) nests, an infrared sensor was used at Cierva\nPoint, Gerlache Strait, in the Antarctic Peninsula. It detects the\nheat emitted by breeding birds beneath debris. Seven other coastal\nareas with an  average surface of 548 m2 were sampled. The infrared\nsensor showed to be highly sensitive to the presence of birds beneath\nrocks; no error sources were found. This method was compared to\nanother conventional method to estimate effectiveness. Besides\nproviding reliable density values, the use of infrared sensor has\nreduced human impact on colonies because no traumatic methods were\nused in order to find nests.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214612175-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.173808
184	Cryology Data from Automatic Recording Stations on James Ross Island, Antarctica	The data includes air, soil, and basement temperatures from various automatic\nrecording stations in Antarctica.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214614014-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.179138
185	Geodetic Data from Automatic Recording Stations in Antarctica	Data sets are comprised of geodetic data from various automatic recording\nstations in Antarctica.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214614114-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.186394
186	The Ordovician System in Antarctica	Ordovician rocks were recognized in the Trans Antarctic Mountains, Ross Orogen, inside the sector claimed by Argentina, and close to this area, in the Ellsworth Mountains. In the Trans Antarctic Mountains, coarse sediments of a probable Ordovician age and radiometrically dated volcanics were recognized. The Pensacola and Patuxent Mounts comprise the Neptune Group, which consists largely of conglomerate to quartzose sandstone. In Shackleton Range the Blaiklock Group overlies the Shackleton Range Metamorphic Complex. Some other areas like Bowers Terrane and Robertson Bay Terrane display remarkable Ordovician outcrops. The Ross Orogen is largely represented with several magmatic exposures.\n\nResumen: EL SISTEMA ORDOVICICO EN ANTARTIDA. \nEn el Territorio Antartico, el Ordovicico ha sido reconocido en localidades en el ambito de los Montes Transantarticos, el denominado orogeno de Ross o Sistema de Ross dentro del sector reclamado por la Republica Argentina y en areas cercanas tales como los montes Ellsworth. En el ambito del los Montes Transantarticos se ha reconocido sedimentos gruesos de probable edad ordovicica y vulcanitas con dataciones radimetricas. En los Montes Pensacola y Patuxent se encuentra el Grupo Neptuno, el cual incluye conglomerados y areniscas cuarzosas. En los Montes Shackleton el Grupo Glaciar Blailock se apoya sobre el Complejo Metamorfico Montes Shackleton. Algo mas alejadas, se han encontrado afloramientos ordovicos en los Terrenis Bowers, y Bahia Robertson. La orogenia Ross se encuentra ampliamente distribuida con presencia de rocas magmaticas.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214613380-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.206074
187	Early and middle Holocene fossil-bearing marine sediments from Potter Peninsula, King George Island (South Shetland Islands, Antarctica)	Holocene sections composed of marine sediments and glacial as well as alluvial\nfan deposits are exposed on the coasts of Potter Peninsula, King George\nIsland/Isla 25 de Mayo, South Shetland Islands (Antarctica) (Fig. 1) (Valle et\nal. 2002, 2003):\n\nSection 1 (Fig. 2): 2.6 m-thick early-to-middle Holocene succession exposed\n(1.17-3.77 m above sea level: a.s.l.) at "Pingfo II" locality (62 degrees 14\nminutes S, 58 degrees 48 minutes W) (Fig. 1) composed of (from base to top): a)\nMarine silty-sands (0.85 m thick: 1.17-2.02 m a.s.l.) deposited in open fjord\nwith Laternula sp shells in growth position, which are radiocarbon dated to c.\n9600 cal yrs BP, b) Glacial-marine deposits (0.15 m thick: 2.02-2.17 m a.s.l.),\nc) Marine beach sands and fine-grained gravels (0.60 m thick: 2.17-2.77 m\na.s.l.) with marine invertebrates, seaweed, and seal and penguin bones (Table\n1) radiocarbon dated to c. 7500 cal yrs BP (Table 2), and d) Glacial till (1 m\nthick: 2.77-3.77 m a.s.l.) covered by a thin coat of marine pebbles (Table 3).\n\nSection 2 (Fig. 3): 2.90 m-thick middle Holocene succession exposed (14.4-17.3\nm a.s.l.) at "Pingfo I" locality (62 degrees 15 minutes S, 58 degrees 37\nminutes W) (Fig. 1) formed of alluvial fan gravels alternated with marine beach\nsands that form three layers between 14.7-16.7 m a.s.l. The raised beach\nsediments contain bones of flying birds, seals, and penguins (Table 1)\nradiocarbon dated to c. 5500 cal yrs BP (Tables 2 and 3).\n\nThese data allow to reconstruct part of the Holocene history of sea level (Fig.\n4) and constrain the age of early-to-middle Holocene environmental changes on\nthe Potter Peninsula coastal area (Table 4): The early Holocene withdrawal of\nglaciers from Potter Cove (south-west King George Island) acompanied by a sea\nlevel high stand allowed marine sedimentation in an open fjord c. 9600 years\nago (Section 1). This high stand of sea level may be related to the 10-9 ka BP\nmarine transgression that drowned the Maxwell Bay coasts (south-west King\nGeorge Island) (Fig. 1) down to 25-30 m depths. After a short-live episode of\nglacial-marine sedimentation, an early-middle Holocene (c. 9600-7500 yr BP)\nlowering of the relative sea level allowed marine beach sediments to be\ndeposited on the fjord sediments (Section 1). The glacial till that covers the\nmarine beach sediments (Section 1) is thought to be deposited during the middle\nHolocene (post-7500 yr BP) glacial advance named "Pu nta Baliza" (PBGA:\nBirkenmajer 1998). The thin coat of raised marine gravels, which covers PBGA\nreworked glacial till at c. 3.77 m a.s.l. (Section 1), suggests that probably\nduring the culmination of PBGA the sea penetrated well into the coastal area of\nPotter Peninsula. Additionally the raised marine beach sediments 14.7-16.7 m\ndated to c. 5500 yr BP (Section 2), confirm that the sea drowned the coasts of\nPotter Peninsula down c. 10-12 m depths during middle Holocene periods of open\nmarine water conditions.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214613608-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.215458
188	Submarine Volcanism in Bransfield Strait, Antarctica	En Espanol:\nEntre 1993 y 1994, como extension de los trabajos volcanologicos y\ngeomorfologicos en el sector noreste de la Peninsula Antartica se\nestudiaron los volcanes submarinos, en parte activos, del estrecho de\nBransfield, "Rifting and Volcanism on the Antarctic Peninsula,\nproyecto de investigacion NSF OPP-8817126 del College of\nOceanography. de la Universidad del Estado de Oregon, EEUU dirigido\npor el Dr. M. Fisk.\n\nIn English:\nBetween 1993 and 1994, as an extension of volcanological and\ngeomorphological studies in the northeast sector of the Antarctic\nPeninsula, researchers studied  partially active submarine volcanoes\nof the Bransfield Strait.  This work is conducted as part of the\nUnited States National Science Foundation Office of Polar Programs\n(NSF OPP) project, Rifting and Volcanism on the Antarctic Peninsula,\nAntarctica (NSF OPP Project No. 8817126).  The project was directed by\nDr. M. Fisk, College of Oceanic & Atmospheric Sciences, Oregon State\nUniversity, USA.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214613402-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.228175
189	EFFECTS OF UV-RADIATION AND HERBIVORY ON PHYSIOLOGY, STRUCTURE SUCCESSION  OF BENTHIC MICRO-MACROALGAL SPECIES OF POTTER COVE (SOUTH SHETLAND, ANTARCTICA)	ENGLISH\n\nDue to the depletion of the stratospheric ozone layer, solar ultraviolet-B\nradiation is increasing on the Earth's surface. UV-B radiation has demonstrated\nto be an environmental stress factor that can cause a harmful effect on the\nphysiology and development of organisms. One of the most particularly affected\nareas is the Antarctic region. The overall objective of this study is the\nestimation of the impact of UV radiation on benthic organisms in Potter Cove\n(South Shetlands, Antarctica). The successional patterns of macro and\nmicroalgal communities will be investigated in field experiments under\ndifferent UV radiation conditions, and in the absence or presence of grazers.\nThe experiments will be carried out on artificial substrata which will be\nplaced in the mesolittoral and sublittoral zones in Potter Cove. This project\nhas started in October 2003 and it is planned to start the activities of the\nsecond year in October 2004.The results will contribute to evaluate the effect\nof UV radiation on the structure and dynamics of the benthic primary producers\ncommunity and to obtain valid information for the preservation of marine\nAntarctic ecosystem.\n\nSPANISH\n\nLa disminucion de la concentracion del ozono estratosferico ha provocado el\nincremento de la radiacion ultravioleta B (UV-B) sobre la superficie de la\ntierra. Esta ultima constituye un factor de estres ambiental que puede danar a\nlos organismos actuando sobre su fisiologia y desarrollo. Una de las regiones\nmas afectadas por este fenomeno es la Antartida. Mediante la implementacion de\neste proyecto se propone el estudio de la incidencia de la radiacion UV sobre\nlos diferentes organismos bentonicos de Caleta Potter (Shetland del Sur,\nAntartida).  Su objetivo es conocer los patrones de colonizacion de las micro y\nmacroalgas  bajo diferentes condiciones de radiacion, en presencia y ausencia\nde herbivoros sobre sustrato artificial mediante la utilizacion de un\nexperimento de sucesion ubicado en el mesolitoral y sublitoral de Caleta Potter\nEl presente proyecto se inicio en Octubre de 2003 y se preve el comienzo del\nsegundo ano para Octubre de 2004. La informacion obtenida contribuira a evaluar\nel efecto de la radiacion UV sobre la estructura y dinamica de la comunidad de\nproductores primarios bentonicos aportando conocimientos para la preservacion\ndel ecosistema marino antartico.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214620700-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:25.238668
196	Extremophiles in Antarctica during 1989-2004	Studies on biodiversity of microorganisms in Antarctica were carried out since\n20 years. Particularly attention was made on those microorganisms defined for\ntheir growth conditions extremophiles. The studies were also devoted to\ncyanobacteria in that these prokaryoted are interesting for the studies on\nearth evolution. Many new extremophiles were isolated belonging to bacteria\ndomain as new species of genus Bacillus, Halomonas, Micrococcus,\nAlicyclobacillus. They were thermophiles and halophilic and alkalophiles.\nStudies on lipid composition and its modulation under stressed growth\nconditions were carried out. Exo- and endopolymers were isolated and studies as\nwell as hydrolytic enzymes.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214620811-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.265845
190	UPPER APTIAN TO UPPER ALBIAN BIOSTRATIGRAPHY OF THE GUSTAV GROUP. AN INTEGRATED STUDY OF THE SEQUENCE EXPOSED IN THE JAMES ROSS ISLAND, ANTARCTICA	ENGLISH\n\nThe investigations will be carried out on the cretaceous outcrops (Aptiano\nsuperior-Albiano), Gustav Group, James Ross Island, Antarctic. The\nstratigraphic sequence and the fossiliferous content will be studied.  The\nbioestratigraphic succession will be determined and ammonites and bivalves\nisotopic analysis (strontium and carbon) will be carried out to establish\ncorrelations with the 87Sr/86Sr and d13C values, known for this period, in the\nNorthern Hemisphere. The results will have importance for the bioestratigraphy\nof this area and to establish a linking with other cretaceous localities of the\nSouthern Hemisphere.\n\nSPANISH\nBioestratigrafia del Aptiano superior-Albiano superior del Grupo Gustav: Un\nestudio integrado de la secuencia expuesta en la Isla James Ross, Antartica\n(PICTA-17-2004).\n\nSe realizaran investigaciones sobre los afloramientos de edad cretacica\n(Aptiano superior-Albiano) del Grupo Gustav, Isla James Ross, Antartica. Se\nestudiara la secuencia estratigrafica y su contenido fosilifero. Se determinara\nla sucesion bioestratigrafica y se realizaran analisis isotopicos de estroncio\ny carbono sobre conchillas de amonites y bivalvos para establecer correlaciones\ncon los valores de  87Sr/86Sr y d13C, conocidos para esta epoca, en el\nhemisferio norte. Los resultados tendran importancia para la bioestratigrafia\nde la region y permitiran establecer una vinculacion con otras localidades\ncretacicas del hemisferio austral.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214620814-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.216073
191	GEOLOGY OF LIVINGSTON AND HALF MOON ISLANDS, SOUTH SHETLAND, ANTARCTICA	ENGLISH\n\nMost of the geological features described for the South Shetlands are sustained on isolated and scattered information (except for King George Island). The goal expected in this project is based on the continuity of the former project and the new obtained data on field information, geochemistry, stable isotopes and geochronology trying to link all into the regional geological and paleogeographical settings. The development of detailed sampling, mapping, profiles and transects will be necessary. Samples will be processed by petrography and selected ones for geochemistry (major, minor and trace elements), geochronology (Rb/Sr, Ar/K) and stable isotopes (18-O and 13-C). Because one of the main problems in the Shetlands is the positioning with elder maps, it is necessary to make corrections, sometimes very deeply, with GPS technology to made accurate maps.  The use of a portable lab for thin sections had demonstrated to be very useful for a better understanding of the lithologies in the field.\n\nSPANISH\n\nLa evolucion geologica de las islas Shetland del Sur por el momento se halla sustentada sobre datos aislados e informacion procedente de observaciones de escasa significacion para las modernas tecnologias aplicadas al campo de la geologia y la tectonica. Se pretende profundizar el conocimiento evolutivo de las Shetland sobre bases geoquimicas,  geocronologicas y observaciones de campo detalladas, con el fin de integrarlas al contexto regional.  Se torna imprescindible el muestreo prolijo, mapeo y construccion de perfiles o transectas en el terreno. Las muestras se analizan petrograficamente y se seleccionan para su procesamiento en laboratorios para analisis quimicos (mayoritarios, minoritarios y traza) e isotopicos (tanto para geoquimica isotopica -Sr, 18-O, 13-C, Nd, como para geocronologia, metodos Rb/Sr, K/Ar). Como apoyo al desarrollo del Proyecto, y ya se ha efectuado anteriormente (isla Media Luna), es menester efectuar un prolijo posicionamiento con GPS de diversos accidentes geograficos.  En cuanto a las tareas de laboratorio en el terreno, la experiencia demuestra que contar con el laboratorio de cortes petrograficos portatil resulta en un mejor conocimiento la litologia en el terreno  su interpretacion, y optimizacion de los muestreos para posteriores analisis.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214620920-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.221126
192	UPPER CRETACEOUS STRATIGRAPHY OF JAMES ROSS BASIN, ANTARCTICA	ENGLISH\n\nEstablishment of a reference stratigraphic and chronologic framework for the\nJames Ross Basin on the basis of the distribution of the ammonite faunas.\nSedimentology and paleoenvironments.\n\nSPANISH\n\nESTRATIGRAFIA DEL CRETACICO SUPERIOR DE LA CUENA JAMES ROSS, ANTARTIDA\n\nTrata de establecer un marco estratigrafico y cronologico de referencia para el\nSantoniano-Maastrichtiano de la Cuenca James Ross en base al estudio de\namonites. Sedimentologia y paleoambientes.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214621346-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.226839
193	Core and Borehole Fractures from the Cape Roberts Drilling Project, Southern Victoria Land, Antarctica: Transantarctic Mountains Front Stress Field History	Natural and drilling-induced fractures in core, and fractures in\n      borehole walls, were logged from three Cape Roberts Project drillholes\n      located in the western Ross Sea, Antarctica.  Core fracture data\n      includes depth, dip and dip azimuth (core coordinates), and\n      fractographic features.  Records were kept of intact core intervals,\n      which are continuous lengths of core where no internal relative motion\n      occurred.  Approximately one quarter of the core from CRP-2 and CRP-3\n      drillholes was oriented by feature matching between oriented borehole\n      televiewer logs and whole-core digital scans.  Tables of rotation\n      angles to restore intact intervals of core to in situ coordinates are\n      available.  Digital records obtained by this project include\n      whole-core scans of most of the core, scans of the entire slabbed\n      core, and borehole televiewer imagery of portions of CRP-2 and CRP-3\n      borehole walls.  The fracture, intact interval and orientation tables\n      are available as Excel files from the project investigators.  The\n      digital core records can be provided on CD-ROM for the cost of the\n      recording media.  Detailed description of the data sets and\n      interpretations made from them can be found in the Cape Roberts\n      Project Intital Reports and Science Reports volumes for each\n      drillhole; see  Reference section.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214611611-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.234676
194	Neodymium, strontium and lead isotope data from onshore and marine tills in Ross Sea and vicinity, Antarctica	This data set includes neodymium, strontium and lead isotope data from the < 63\n      micron size fraction from both onshore and marine surface tills from the Ross\n      Sea and vicinity.  From western Antarctica, onshore tills were analyzed\n      associated with the Whillans, Kamb and MacAyeal ice streams.  In eastern\n      Antarctica, onshore tills from the Beardmore and Darwin glaciers were analyzed,\n      and from Mt. Archenar and the Allan Hills.  Marine tills from the RISP cores\n      and from numerous cores throughout the Ross Sea were analyzed.	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214614915-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.239291
195	Diagnostic Precipitation Data of Antarctica	In this gridded dataset, the precipitation for Antarctica from 1979-99\n      has been retrieved by a vertical motion calculation using ECMWF\n      operational analyses (ECT, 1985-1999; twice daily) and reanalysis\n      (ERA-15, 1979-1993; 4 times daily). The use of the dynamic retrieval\n      method to calculate precipitation has been developed and verified for\n      the Greenland ice sheet.  Most major features in the spatial\n      distribution of Antarctic accumulation are well captured by the\n      dynamic retrieval results. In comparison with predicted precipitation\n      amounts from ERA-15 (1979-1993), ECT (1991-1999), and National Centers\n      for Environmental Prediction (NCEP) / Department of Energy,\n      Atmospheric Model Intercomparison Project Version 2 reanalysis\n      (1979-1999), dynamic retrieval calculations capture more mesoscale\n      features of the precipitation distribution over Antarctica. In\n      comparison to available glaciological measurements, the interannual\n      variability of the Antarctic precipitation is well represented by the\n      dynamic retrieval results at those measurement sites. A significant\n      upward trend of +1.2 to +1.7 mm yr-2 is found from retrieved and\n      forecast precipitation amounts for Antarctica that is consistent with\n      results reported by other investigators.\n      \n      Twenty-two sigma levels are used in the vertical, and a 40-km grid\n      size is used in the horizontal. The square model domain used in this\n      study consists of 181 grid points in each orthogonal direction,\n      centered at the South Pole, with a horizontal polar stereographic\n      projection resolution of 40 km. The model topography data over the\n      Antarctic continent are also interpolated from a 5-km resolution\n      digital elevation model using bi-linear interpolation from the nearest\n      points.  This dataset, as well as references to methodology and\n      validation, are currently available upon request from the Polar\n      Meteorology Group, Byrd Polar Research Center, Columbus, OH. Email\n      David Bromwich (bromwich@polarmet1.mps.ohio-state.edu).	dataset	\N	NASA Earthdata	C1214611890-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.258016
197	Vector Topographic Data of Antarctica - The Antarctic Digital Database	The Antarctic Digital Database (ADD) is the premier source of vector topographic data for Antarctica. First published on CD-ROM in 1993, the current version is available for download from http://www.add.scar.org/. The data are supplied in ARC/INFO uncompressed export format, compressed and encrypted for download using zip. The data are encrypted using an individual password which is supplied to registered users of the ADD site.  There is no charge for registration as a user. The aim of registration is to allow British Antarctic Survey (BAS) to contact the user if there are improvements in the data which may be of interest. It also ensures that BAS maintain the intellectual property rights on the data for the Scientific Committee on Antarctic Research (SCAR).  The data on the ADD site are available free of charge to research or non-profit organizations. Use of these data for commercial purposes is prohibited without prior authorization from SCAR. Those wishing to\n  use the data for commercial purposes must contact SCAR, who will enter into appropriate licensing arrangements.	dataset	http://www.add.scar.org/	NASA Earthdata	C1214594511-SCIOPS	\N	\N	2026-09-20 21:52:26.270888
